T* () Tj
T* (The District Court's order was accompanied by an) Tj
T* (opinion which provides the framework for this appeal, at) Tj
T* (the heart of which lay a number of conclusions. First, the) Tj
T* (Court held that the case was governed by the test) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (                                3) Tj
T* () Tj
T* () Tj
T* (developed in Richmond Newspapers, Inc. v. Virginia, 448) Tj
T* (U.S. 555 \(1980\), a murder case in which the trial judge had) Tj
T* (ordered that the courtroom be cleared of all persons except) Tj
T* (witnesses. In striking down the closure order, the Supreme) Tj
T* (Court noted an "unbroken, uncontradicted history" of) Tj
T* (public access to criminal trials in Anglo American law) Tj
T* (running from "before the Norman Conquest" to the present.) Tj
T* (It emphasized that it had not found "a single instance of a) Tj
T* (criminal trial conducted in camera in any federal, state, or) Tj
T* (municipal court during the history of this country." Id. at) Tj
T* (565-73. The Supreme Court held that the right of the press) Tj
T* (and public to attend criminal trials "is implicit in the) Tj
T* (guarantees of the First Amendment." Id. at 580. While the) Tj
T* (Court acknowledged the State's argument that the) Tj
T* (Constitution nowhere explicitly guarantees the public's) Tj
T* (right to attend criminal trials, it nonetheless held the right) Tj
T* (implicit due to the fact that the Framers drafted the) Tj
T* (Constitution against a backdrop of popular access.) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (In its opinion in this case, the District Court rejected the) Tj
T* (Government's argument that administrative hearings in) Tj
T* (general, and deportation hearings in particular, are not) Tj
T* (subject to the Richmond Newspapers two-part"experience) Tj
T* (and logic" test because they are of a fundamentally different) Tj
T* (nature. Instead, the Court applied that test, which asks) Tj
T* (first whether a particular proceeding has a history of) Tj
T* (openness, and then whether openness plays a positive role) Tj
T* (in that proceeding. With respect to the experience inquiry,) Tj
T* (the District Court relied especially on a line of Third Circuit) Tj
T* (cases which has applied Richmond Newspapers to find) Tj
T* (access to a number of auxiliary criminal proceedings, as) Tj
T* (well as to civil cases. The Court also relied on two cases in) Tj
T* (which we applied Richmond Newspapers to determine) Tj
T* (whether access should be granted to administrative) Tj
T* (proceedings, although we concluded in each instance that) Tj
T* (there was no access. In short, the District Court reasoned) Tj
T* (that these cases supported application of Richmond) Tj
T* (Newspapers, and, applying Richmond Newspapers, found) Tj
T* (that there was a sufficient history of open deportation) Tj
T* (proceedings to satisfy the Richmond Newspapers  experience) Tj
T* (test.) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (Turning to the logic prong, the District Court held that) Tj
T* (policy considerations strongly favored media access.) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (                                4) Tj
T* () Tj
T* () Tj
T* (Significantly, however, in evaluating the logic prong, the) Tj
T* (Court did not consider the policies militating against media) Tj
T* (access, including those identified in a declaration filed by) Tj
