T* (Dale Watson, Counterterrorism Chief of the Federal Bureau) Tj
T* (of Investigation, which explained the danger of security) Tj
T* (breaches entailed in opening the hearings. In brief, the) Tj
T* (Watson Declaration represents that insight gleaned from) Tj
T* (open proceedings might alert vigilant terrorists to the) Tj
T* (United States' investigative tactics and could easily betray) Tj
T* (what knowledge the government does -- or does not--) Tj
T* (possess. Watson submits that even details that seem) Tj
T* (innocuous in isolation, such as the names of those) Tj
T* (detained, might be pieced together by knowledgeable) Tj
T* (persons within the terrorist network, who could in turn) Tj
T* (shift activities to a yet-undiscovered terrorist cell. Because) Tj
T* (immigration judges cannot be expected accurately to assess) Tj
T* (the harm that might result from disclosing seemingly trivial) Tj
T* (facts, Watson explains, seeking closure on a case-by-case) Tj
T* (basis would ineffectively protect the nation's interests.) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (Although existing caselaw on the logic prong has) Tj
T* (discussed only the policies favoring openness, we are) Tj
T* (satisfied that the logic prong must consider the flip side of) Tj
T* (the coin. Indeed, the Supreme Court seems to have) Tj
T* (contemplated this, for in formulating the Richmond) Tj
T* (Newspapers test it asked "whether public access plays a) Tj
T* (significant positive role in the functioning of the particular) Tj
T* (process in question." Press-Enterprise Co. v. Superior Court,) Tj
T* (478 U.S. 1, 8 \(1986\) \(emphasis added\). Any inquiry into) Tj
T* (whether a role is positive must perforce consider whether it) Tj
T* (is potentially harmful. The District Court, however, failed to) Tj
T* (consider the Watson Declaration under its logic inquiry,) Tj
T* (examining it only in conjunction with the Newspapers') Tj
T* (argument that the Creppy Directive failed strict scrutiny, a) Tj
T* (position that it endorsed.) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (While we believe that the notion that Richmond) Tj
T* (Newspapers applies is open to debate as a theoretical) Tj
T* (matter, we must yield to the prior precedent of this Court,) Tj
T* (and hence will apply it to the facts. We note, however, that) Tj
T* (we are not bound by dicta in those decisions, including the) Tj
T* (most far reaching, Whiteland Woods, L.P. v. Township of) Tj
T* (West Whiteland, 193 F.3d 177 \(3d Cir. 1999\), which we) Tj
T* (discuss at length infra.) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (                                5) Tj
T* () Tj
T* () Tj
T* (The only Circuit to deal with these issues has resolved) Tj
T* (them in favor of the media. See Detroit Free Press v.) Tj
T* (Ashcroft, 2002 U.S. App. LEXIS 17646 \(6th Cir. 2002\).) Tj
T* (However, we find ourselves in disagreement with the Sixth) Tj
T* (Circuit. In our view the tradition of openness of deportation) Tj
T* (proceedings does not meet the standard required by) Tj
T* (Richmond Newspapers, or even its Third Circuit progeny.) Tj
T* (Deportation procedures have been codified for) Tj
T* (approximately 100 years but, despite their constant) Tj
T* (reenactment during that time, Congress has never explicitly) Tj
T* (guaranteed public access. Indeed, deportation cases) Tj
T* (involving abused alien children are mandatorily closed by) Tj
T* (statute, and hearings are often conducted in places) Tj
T* (generally inaccessible to the public. While INS regulations) Tj
T* (promulgated in 1964 create a rebuttable presumption of) Tj
