T* (openness for most deportation cases, we conclude that a) Tj
T* (recently-created regulatory presumption of openness with) Tj
T* (significant statutory exceptions does not present the type of) Tj
T* ("unbroken, uncontradicted history" that Richmond) Tj
T* (Newspapers and its progeny require to establish a First) Tj
T* (Amendment right of access.) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (The most difficult case for the government is FMC v.) Tj
T* (South Carolina State Ports Authority, 122 S. Ct. 1864) Tj
T* (\(2002\). In holding that state sovereign immunity bars an) Tj
T* (administrative agency from adjudicating a private party's) Tj
T* (complaint against a nonconsenting state, the Supreme) Tj
T* (Court recognized that "formalized administrative) Tj
T* (adjudications were all but unheard of " during the Framers') Tj
T* (time. Id. at 1872. It nevertheless found that because) Tj
T* (Federal Maritime Commission adjudications so strongly) Tj
T* (resemble civil suits, they "are the type of proceedings from) Tj
T* (which the Framers would have thought the States) Tj
T* (possessed immunity when they agreed to enter the Union,") Tj
T* (id., and it concluded that state sovereign immunity applies.) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (We recognize that, at least since the 1960s, formalized) Tj
T* (deportation proceedings have borne an undeniable) Tj
T* (procedural resemblance to civil trials, and that, read) Tj
T* (broadly, Ports Authority's language might therefore suggest) Tj
T* (that the same First Amendment rights exist in each) Tj
T* (context. While we find the issue debatable, as we explain) Tj
T* (more extensively infra, we believe that Ports Authority's) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (                                6) Tj
T* () Tj
T* () Tj
T* (approach was inextricably tied to its underlying premise) Tj
T* (that sovereign immunity shields nonconsenting states from) Tj
T* (complaints brought by private persons, regardless of where) Tj
T* (private persons bring those complaints. In contrast, we find) Tj
T* (that there has never been a fundamental right of access to) Tj
T* (all government proceedings. Even today, many are closed) Tj
T* (by statute, including such frequent and important matters) Tj
T* (as Social Security hearings. Without a fundamental right of) Tj
T* (access comparable to nonconsenting states' right to) Tj
T* (freedom from private claims, we decline to loose Ports) Tj
T* (Authority from its Eleventh Amendment moorings.) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (We also disagree with the Sixth Circuit as to the import) Tj
T* (of the Richmond Newspapers logic prong. We note) Tj
T* (preliminarily that, in the jurisprudence developed thus far,) Tj
T* (the logic prong does not appear to do much work in the) Tj
T* (Richmond Newspapers approach, for we have not found a) Tj
T* (case in which a proceeding passed the experience test) Tj
T* (through its history of openness yet failed the logic test by) Tj
T* (not serving community values. Under the reported cases,) Tj
T* (the second prong of the Richmond Newspapers test has) Tj
T* (been applied to inquire whether openness plays a positive) Tj
T* (policy role in a given proceeding. But, as we have explained,) Tj
T* (that calculus perforce must take account of the flip side --) Tj
T* (the extent to which openness impairs the public good.) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (This case arises in the wake of September 11, 2001, a) Tj
T* (day on which American life changed drastically and) Tj
