T* (criminal prosecutions. 18 U.S.C. App. 3 SS 2-4, 6, 8. These proceedings) Tj
T* (may be held in camera and, in certain circumstances, ex parte. Id. SS 2-) Tj
T* (4, 6; United States v. Kilmavicius-Viloria, 144 F.3d 1249, 1260-61 \(9th) Tj
T* (Cir. 1998\). Congress also has created the Foreign Intelligence) Tj
T* (Surveillance Court to hold closed reviews of search warrant requests on) Tj
T* (a case-by-case basis, rejecting a framework where the Department of) Tj
T* (Justice makes its own judgments on these matters in national security) Tj
T* (cases. 50 U.S.C. S 1801 et seq.) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (                                48) Tj
T* () Tj
T* () Tj
T* (deference, that the apparently innocuous information) Tj
T* (provides appropriate grounds for closure.) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (The Watson Declaration also expresses the fear that open) Tj
T* (deportation hearings could provide evidence to terrorists) Tj
T* (that certain border crossings offer a greater chance for) Tj
T* (illegal entry than others. At oral argument, government) Tj
T* (counsel offered an intriguing hypothetical where open) Tj
T* (hearings would reveal evidence that 0-of-30 terrorists had) Tj
T* (entered the United States successfully through) Tj
T* (Philadelphia, while the rate was 30-of-30 in New York City.) Tj
T* (Here too, however, the government could make this) Tj
T* (argument during a closed preliminary hearing at which the) Tj
T* (Immigration Judge, with appropriate deference to national) Tj
T* (security, could assess the government's concerns about) Tj
T* (publicizing patterns of information.) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (The Creppy Directive and the pre-existing Department of) Tj
T* (Justice regulations both accommodate the government's) Tj
T* (national security responsibilities. But a case-by-case) Tj
T* (approach would permit an Immigration Judge to) Tj
T* (independently assess the balance of these fundamental) Tj
T* (values.16 Because this is a reasonable alternative, the) Tj
T* (Creppy Directive's blanket closure rule is constitutionally) Tj
T* (infirm.17 As the Supreme Court reasoned in Globe) Tj
T* (Newspaper:) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (       We emphasize that our holding is a narrow one: that a) Tj
T* (       rule of mandatory closure . . . is constitutionally) Tj
T* (       infirm. In individual cases, and under appropriate) Tj
T* (       circumstances, the First Amendment does not) Tj
T* (       necessarily stand as a bar to the exclusion from the) Tj
T* (       courtroom of the press and general public . . . . But a) Tj
