T* (16. As noted, the Department of Justice regulations permit an) Tj
T* (Immigration Judge to close deportation hearings in their entirety in order) Tj
T* (to protect the public interest. This closure is broader in scope than what) Tj
T* (is likely to occur in criminal prosecutions because immigration) Tj
T* (proceedings call for greater deference to the government on matters of) Tj
T* (national security.) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (17. As noted, the Supreme Court held in Press-Enterprise II that a) Tj
T* (restriction on a qualified right of access fails if there are "reasonable) Tj
T* (alternatives to closure." 478 U.S. at 14.) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (                                49) Tj
T* () Tj
T* () Tj
T* (       mandatory rule, requiring no particularized) Tj
T* (       determinations in individual cases, is unconstitutional.) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (457 U.S. at 611 n.27.) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (The stakes are high. Cherished traditions of openness) Tj
T* (have come up against the vital and compelling imperatives) Tj
T* (of national security. Because I believe national security) Tj
T* (interests can be fully accommodated on a case-by-case) Tj
T* (basis, I would affirm that part of the District Court's) Tj
T* (judgment.) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (IV. Nationwide Injunction) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (The final issue is whether the scope of the District) Tj
T* (Court's nationwide injunction is overbroad. The grant of a) Tj
T* (permanent injunction is reviewed for abuse of discretion.) Tj
T* (Nutrasweet Co. v. Vit-Mar Enters., Inc., 176 F.3d 151, 153) Tj
T* (\(3d Cir. 1999\). I would hold that the District Court abused) Tj
T* (its discretion by issuing a nationwide injunction that bars) Tj
T* (enforcement of the Creppy Directive in any special interest) Tj
T* (proceeding and against any member of the general public) Tj
T* (or press. A narrower remedy will provide full relief to) Tj
T* (plaintiffs and allow other courts to explore this difficult) Tj
T* (constitutional question.) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (Generally, a plaintiff is only entitled to relief for itself.) Tj
T* (Ameron, Inc. v. U.S. Army Corps of Eng'rs, 787 F.2d 875,) Tj
T* (888 \(3d Cir. 1986\). In the First Amendment context,) Tj
T* ("although the occasional case requires us to entertain a) Tj
T* (facial challenge in order to vindicate a party's right not to) Tj
T* (be bound by an unconstitutional statute, we neither want) Tj
T* (nor need to provide relief to nonparties when a narrower) Tj
T* (remedy will fully protect the litigants." United States v. Nat'l) Tj
T* (Treasury Employees Union, 513 U.S. 454, 477-78 \(1995\)) Tj
T* (\(citation omitted\). In this case, injunctive relief should) Tj
T* (apply only to those parties actually before the court if that) Tj
T* (relief fully protects the litigants. See Ameron , 787 F.2d at) Tj
T* (890.) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (Furthermore, where the government is involved in) Tj
T* (litigation, a court should not "thwart the development of) Tj
T* (important questions of law by freezing the first final) Tj
T* (decision rendered on a particular legal issue." United States) Tj
T* (v. Mendoza, 464 U.S. 154, 160 \(1984\). Because) Tj
T* () Tj
